@h1 Layout — rows & columns h1@

Sometimes you want two things side by side: an image next to its explanation, a
table beside a note, two columns of commentary. Wit gives you an invisible band —
a row — that lays its columns out horizontally. No floats, no CSS, and it
degrades gracefully: on a narrow screen or in print, the columns simply stack.

@h2 The shape h2@

A row contains columns. Put whatever you like in each column — prose, an image,
a table:

@@@pre
@row
@col |size 260|
@img |src ./lamp.png| |alt The keeper's lamp|
col@
@col
The keeper trimmed the wick at dusk, as he had every evening for eleven years,
and set the lamp turning against the dark.
col@
row@
pre@@@

The first column is fixed at 260 pixels wide; the second has no size, so it fills
whatever space is left. That "one fixed, one flexible" pattern is the workhorse
for an image beside its caption-length explanation.

@h2 Two columns of prose h2@

Two bare columns — neither given a size — split the row evenly. Here it is live:

@row
@col
_The lamp room._ Glass on every side, and a lens the size of a barrel that threw
the light twenty miles out to the shipping lanes.
col@
@col
_The watch room._ Below the light, a desk, a barometer, and the log in which
every hour of every night was accounted for.
col@
row@

@h2 Sizing a column h2@

A column's size parameter accepts the same vocabulary as an image: a named preset
(small, medium, large — 220, 380, and 600 pixels), a plain pixel number, or a
percentage. A column _without_ a size fills the space that the sized columns
leave:

@ul
@li _A fixed column_ — @@@code @col |size 260| code@@@ — holds a set width. li@
@li _A flexible column_ — a bare @@@code @col code@@@ — grows to fill what
remains. li@
@li _Several flexible columns_ share the leftover space equally. li@
ul@

@h2 It wraps on narrow widths h2@

The row is set to wrap, so when the viewport (or the printed page) is too narrow
to hold the columns side by side, they stack vertically instead. You get a
two-column layout on a wide screen and a readable single column on a phone,
without writing a media query.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _A row buried in a paragraph._ A row placed directly after prose can get
wrapped in a paragraph, which is invalid nesting that browsers merely tolerate.
Separate a row from surrounding prose with blank lines so it stays a top-level
block. li@
@li _A column with no row._ A column outside a row still renders a box, but
without the flex band around it the side-by-side layout never happens. Always
pair columns inside a row. li@
ul@

@h2 See also h2@

@ul
